 ## [Unreleased]
 
+### Fixed — time & timezone correctness (2026-11-15, 08:00 IST pass)
+- **A corrupt `utc_offset_seconds` took the whole app down to "Offline".** Every
+  time helper in the page reads the city's clock from the payload's own
+  `utc_offset_seconds` applied to the device's UTC clock, and that field was
+  taken on trust: it was accepted if it was a finite number and nothing else.
+  An out-of-range value reached `new Date(...)` in `cityNow()`, whose
+  `toISOString()` throws a `RangeError` on an instant it cannot represent —
+  and that call is `w.fetchedDate = cityDate(w)`, two lines *before* the payload
+  is written to the cache. `show()` catches a throw as a *link* failure, so a
+  complete forecast was replaced by the "Offline — check your internet
+  connection" card, the payload was never cached, and the retry card re-fetched
+  the same payload and failed in exactly the same way, for good. The value is
+  now accepted only if it is an offset a place can actually have (±14:00, the
+  real range is −12:00 … +14:00); anything else is ignored exactly as a missing
+  field always has been, so the page falls back to the payload's own clock — the
+  path a payload without `utc_offset_seconds` has always taken — and the date is
+  checked for representability before it is converted, so no other route to an
+  unrepresentable instant can throw out of there either. This is the same class
+  of defect as the missing `uv_index_max` (1.6.1) and the truncated hourly block
+  (1.6.3): a payload field the renderer trusts and `usableForecast()` does not
+  check. Every other field the time path reads was re-audited here and is
+  already guarded.
+- Nothing on screen changes: with a valid offset (every real timezone) the
+  rendered page is byte-identical to 1.12. Verified with `node --check` on the
+  page's script; a headless Chromium load of `web/index.html` with mocked
+  forecast, geocoder and Commons responses reporting **zero console errors and
+  zero page errors**; and the fix proved by loading the same payload twice —
+  with `utc_offset_seconds` 1e15 and −1e15 the 1.12 page draws the "Offline"
+  card with nothing cached, while this build draws the forecast and caches it,
+  and that render is identical to the same payload with the field removed.
+  Seven payloads (IST, −05:00 New York at night, +12:45 Chatham, +14:00, no
+  offset at all, a night thunderstorm, and the default) produce an identical
+  `#main`, identical computed styles for the bar, hero, cards, strip, day rows
+  and pill, and identical `--k/--glass/--blur/--dp/--pr/--cc` against 1.12.
+  The time path itself was re-exercised and is unchanged: a payload fetched at
+  23:50 and read at 00:05 still moves "Today" to the new day and opens the strip
+  on 12 AM, the post-midnight API-lag payload still labels its rows
+  `Sun, Today, Tue…`, a London spring-forward night still reads 1 AM → 3 AM, and
+  the rendered output is identical under device timezones IST, America/New_York
+  and Pacific/Kiritimati for the same city.
+- Version bumped to 1.12.1 (`versionCode 73`), same signing key, so it installs
+  over 1.12; `android/assets/index.html` is byte-identical to `web/index.html`
+  (SHA-256 `d511bd24…`), the APK rebuilt from it bundles that same page and
+  carries `versionCode 73` / `versionName 1.12.1` with v1, v2 and v3 signatures
+  verifying against the project key (`2035d69f…`, `CN=Liquid Weather`), and
+  `CHECKSUMS.txt` and the README carry the new digest (`ca13bddc…`). The APK is
+  still 105 KB, so no size figure moved.
+- Left alone deliberately: (1) the three items the 11 November pass recorded —
+  a DST transition inside the ten-minute cache window, "tonight"/"overnight"
+  wording in the small hours (visible copy, and the UI is frozen), and a payload
+  more than seven days old — were re-checked and are unchanged: the first is at
+  most ten minutes of one hour's error and needs a timezone database the app
+  deliberately does not carry, the second is a wording decision rather than a
+  bug, and the third needs a page left alive and offline for a week. (2) A
+  device clock that is wrong by a few hours *within* the same date still drags
+  the hourly strip to the device's hour, because a clock three hours ahead and a
+  payload three hours stale look identical from inside the page; the day-long
+  disagreement is already distrusted, and the alternative would break the
+  midnight correction that is the reason the city clock is read at all. (3) An
+  hourly or daily block whose `time` entries are not strings at all (`null`)
+  still throws in the label formatters; no API and no truncation produces that
+  — a cut body fails `JSON.parse` first — so it is written down rather than
+  guessed at.
+
